WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.327
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.627 --> 00:00:09.443
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:16.714
Unit fourteen: Global Political Economy. Today you will weigh effects using benefit/drawback contrast structures.

4
00:00:17.014 --> 00:00:19.574
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:24.056
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.917 --> 00:00:26.359
Political economy.

7
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:31.683
Political economy means the study of how politics and economics shape each other.

8
00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:35.247
Political economy asks who gains from a policy.

9
00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.955
Your turn. Say: Political economy.

10
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.149
Political economy.

11
00:00:43.883 --> 00:00:45.442
Global trade regime.

12
00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:51.352
Global trade regime means the rules and institutions governing international trade.

13
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:55.018
The global trade regime reduced tariffs worldwide.

14
00:00:55.617 --> 00:00:59.233
Your turn. Say: Global trade regime.

15
00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:03.997
Global trade regime.

16
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:05.902
Capital flow.

17
00:01:06.917 --> 00:01:10.762
Capital flow means the movement of money for investment across borders.

18
00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:14.348
Sudden capital flows destabilised the currency.

19
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.020
Your turn. Say: Capital flow.

20
00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:22.100
Capital flow.

21
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:23.851
Development.

22
00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:29.125
Development means improvement in incomes, health and living standards.

23
00:01:29.717 --> 00:01:31.786
Development requires more than growth.

24
00:01:32.383 --> 00:01:35.094
Your turn. Say: Development.

25
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:38.923
Development.

26
00:01:39.683 --> 00:01:40.824
Inequality.

27
00:01:41.817 --> 00:01:46.603
Inequality means unequal distribution of income, wealth or opportunity.

28
00:01:47.217 --> 00:01:49.681
Inequality widened within many countries.

29
00:01:50.283 --> 00:01:52.762
Your turn. Say: Inequality.

30
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:56.694
Inequality.

31
00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.287
Multinational corporation.

32
00:02:00.283 --> 00:02:04.849
Multinational corporation means a company operating in several countries.

33
00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.889
A multinational corporation moved production overseas.

34
00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:12.623
Your turn. Say: Multinational corporation.

35
00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:17.582
Multinational corporation.

36
00:02:18.317 --> 00:02:20.026
Regulatory framework.

37
00:02:21.017 --> 00:02:25.234
Regulatory framework means the set of rules governing an industry.

38
00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:28.744
A regulatory framework protects consumers.

39
00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:32.664
Your turn. Say: Regulatory framework.

40
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.325
Regulatory framework.

41
00:02:38.083 --> 00:02:39.793
Economic interdependence.

42
00:02:40.783 --> 00:02:45.256
Economic interdependence means mutual reliance of economies on one another.

43
00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:49.266
Economic interdependence links producers and consumers.

44
00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:53.385
Your turn. Say: Economic interdependence.

45
00:02:56.233 --> 00:02:58.001
Economic interdependence.

46
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.149
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:02.983 --> 00:03:10.373
Visal, my uncle's factory exports everywhere, but a distant crisis nearly ruined him. Is trade a blessing or a curse?

48
00:03:11.483 --> 00:03:16.260
Both. Interdependence brings growth but also vulnerability.

49
00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:19.048
Can you give a clearer picture?

50
00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:32.195
On the benefit side, capital flows fund development; on the drawback side, they can reverse suddenly. Trade raises average incomes, yet it also widens inequality.

51
00:03:33.283 --> 00:03:35.004
So what should governments do?

52
00:03:36.117 --> 00:03:43.343
Not retreat, but regulate. A sound regulatory framework keeps the gains while limiting the risks.

53
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.082
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:46.917 --> 00:03:50.716
Grammar focus: Benefit and drawback contrast structures.

55
00:03:51.016 --> 00:03:52.191
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:52.883 --> 00:03:55.928
Interdependence brings growth but also vulnerability.

57
00:03:56.817 --> 00:03:59.966
Trade raises average incomes, yet it can widen inequality.

58
00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:04.452
While capital flows fund development, they can reverse suddenly.

59
00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:14.426
Balance gains and costs with brings X but also Y, yet, while, although. Keep parallel form: growth but also vulnerability (noun + noun).

60
00:04:15.183 --> 00:04:19.066
Be careful! Don't say: Trade brings growth but also vulnerable.

61
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.335
Say: Trade brings growth but also vulnerability.

62
00:04:24.635 --> 00:04:26.251
Use a noun after 'also'.

63
00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:29.812
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.518
The Price and Prize of Openness.

65
00:04:32.818 --> 00:04:37.349
Global political economy examines how trade, finance and power interact.

66
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:44.721
Interdependence brings growth but also vulnerability, since a crisis in one region can spread rapidly to others.

67
00:04:45.733 --> 00:04:51.019
While capital flows fund development, they can reverse suddenly and destabilise currencies.

68
00:04:52.033 --> 00:04:59.223
Multinational corporations spread technology and jobs, yet they can also exploit weak regulatory frameworks.

69
00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:07.968
Trade raises average incomes, though it often widens inequality, so policy must distribute gains and compensate losers.

70
00:05:09.067 --> 00:05:13.092
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:17.343
Interdependence brings growth blank also vulnerability.

72
00:05:17.643 --> 00:05:20.739
A, but. B, so. C, or.

73
00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.033
The answer is A: but.

74
00:05:27.183 --> 00:05:30.588
The movement of money across borders for investment is blank.

75
00:05:30.888 --> 00:05:34.889
A, capital flow. B, development. C, inequality.

76
00:05:38.433 --> 00:05:40.410
The answer is A: capital flow.

77
00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.699
Unequal distribution of income or wealth is blank.

78
00:05:44.999 --> 00:05:49.198
A, regime. B, inequality. C, framework.

79
00:05:52.733 --> 00:05:55.012
The answer is B: inequality.

80
00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:57.766
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:58.066 --> 00:06:05.644
A, Trade brings jobs but also risky.. B, Trade brings jobs but also risk.. C, Trade brings jobs but risk also..

82
00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.999
The answer is B: Trade brings jobs but also risk..

83
00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:16.486
According to the reading, policy must…

84
00:06:16.786 --> 00:06:22.831
A, distribute gains and compensate losers. B, stop trade. C, ignore inequality.

85
00:06:26.367 --> 00:06:29.910
The answer is A: distribute gains and compensate losers.

86
00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:34.089
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:39.144
What is one benefit and one drawback of global economic interdependence?

88
00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:57.427
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:57.727 --> 00:07:22.244
One benefit is access to larger markets and cheaper goods, which raises living standards. One drawback is vulnerability: a shock in one country, such as a financial crisis or supply disruption, can quickly spread elsewhere. Interdependence brings opportunity but also risk, so countries need diversification and good regulation.

90
00:07:22.544 --> 00:07:24.450
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:30.719
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and benefit and drawback contrast structures.

92
00:07:31.019 --> 00:07:36.574
Today's value is global economic literacy. Understand how money and trade connect the world.

93
00:07:36.874 --> 00:07:39.573
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:39.873 --> 00:07:42.130
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

